<gpx version="1.1" creator="TrailMind research build" xmlns="http://www.topografix.com/GPX/1/1" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.topografix.com/GPX/1/1 http://www.topografix.com/GPX/1/1/gpx.xsd">
  <metadata>
    <name>Forest of Dean: hidden places from Lydney</name>
    <desc>63 lesser-known places in the Forest of Dean and lower Wye, found in local-history society records, forums (Derelict Places, Geograph), heritage lists and OpenStreetMap, checked against OS maps. Waypoint text says what to look for, the source of the position and any access limits. Some sites are on private land or open seasonally. Never enter mines, levels or tunnels, and keep back from scowles, shafts and cliff edges. Line: BRouter hiking-mountain on OpenStreetMap paths (walk or drive between clusters); straight end pieces reach spots just off the mapped paths.</desc>
    <time>2026-09-27T12:37:22Z</time>
    <keywords>Forest of Dean, Lydney, mines, air shafts, tramroads, railways, WWII, folklore</keywords>
    <bounds minlat="51.641338" minlon="-2.674121" maxlat="51.870479" maxlon="-2.423351"/>
  </metadata>
  <wpt lat="51.725960" lon="-2.532630">
    <ele>31</ele>
    <name>Pidcock's Canal</name>
    <desc>Stop 1. [Industrial] Hidden in Lydney: a Georgian canal, built by 1790, linking the Upper, Middle and Lower forges. The Pidcocks, Staffordshire glassmasters who ran the forges from 1790 to 1813, used it to carry coal down to Lydney Pill. The Severn &amp; Wye tramroad later ran beside it and crossed it on two drawbridges; it fell out of use in the 1840s, but stretches of the channel survive along the Newerne valley.</desc>
    <link href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pidcock%27s_Canal"><text>Wikipedia: Pidcock's Canal</text></link>
    <sym>Water Source</sym>
    <type>Industrial</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="51.735990" lon="-2.538510">
    <ele>30</ele>
    <name>Norchard red drain</name>
    <desc>Stop 2. [Mining] Water from old colliery workings between Norchard and Pillowell gushes into the River Lyd here at about 20,000 litres a minute, summer and winter, stained bright ochre-red by iron oxide. It is beside the Dean Forest Railway's Norchard station, on the site of the old colliery and power station. Slippery banks; keep off the railway.</desc>
    <link href="https://www.geograph.org.uk/photo/896174"><text>Geograph 896174</text></link>
    <sym>Water Source</sym>
    <type>Mining</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="51.760880" lon="-2.547680">
    <ele>111</ele>
    <name>Oakenhill scowles</name>
    <desc>Stop 3. [Mining] Scowles, the Forest's ancient opencast iron workings, in Oakenhill Wood above Yorkley, with a puzzle: small stones on their rims each carry a single letter (K, E and others). They aren't usually counted as gale stones, and some are said to be orienteering stones, but numbered gale stones 108 and 75 nearby mark genuine 1840s free-miner claims. Steep sides and holes; don't move the stones.</desc>
    <link href="https://www.geograph.org.uk/photo/8003550"><text>Geograph 8003550</text></link>
    <link href="https://www.geograph.org.uk/photo/7959898"><text>Geograph 7959898</text></link>
    <sym>Mine</sym>
    <type>Mining</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="51.775450" lon="-2.582170">
    <ele>168</ele>
    <name>Gale stone No. 77</name>
    <desc>Stop 4. [Mining] A stone on Birch Hill inscribed '77' marks a coal 'gale', the mining claim granted to a Forest free miner by the Verderers' Court in 1835. Free miners, people born in the Hundred of St Briavels who have worked a year and a day in a mine, still hold these rights. A capped shaft in a clearing at RSPB Nagshead nearby may be an old air shaft or a well.</desc>
    <link href="https://www.geograph.org.uk/photo/7980776"><text>Geograph 7980776</text></link>
    <link href="https://www.geograph.org.uk/photo/1318925"><text>Geograph 1318925</text></link>
    <sym>Flag, Red</sym>
    <type>Mining</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="51.776439" lon="-2.595560">
    <ele>152</ele>
    <name>Darkhill &amp; Titanic works</name>
    <desc>Stop 5. [Industrial] Ruins where David Mushet ran an ironworks from 1818 and his son Robert Forester Mushet experimented. Robert's 1856 trick of adding spiegeleisen made Bessemer steel workable, and in 1868 he made 'Mushet steel', the first commercial self-hardening tool steel. Scheduled monument beside the Family Cycle Trail near Milkwall.</desc>
    <link href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/node/7167337456"><text>OpenStreetMap node 7167337456</text></link>
    <link href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Darkhill_Ironworks"><text>Wikipedia: Darkhill Ironworks</text></link>
    <sym>Ruins</sym>
    <type>Industrial</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="51.788456" lon="-2.626782">
    <ele>166</ele>
    <name>Whitecliff furnace</name>
    <desc>Stop 6. [Industrial] Shell of an early coke-fired blast furnace, built around 1800 to smelt Forest iron ore with coke instead of charcoal. It never really paid; David Mushet (Robert's father) was involved before it was abandoned early in the 19th century. Consolidated ruins you can walk round, just off the Coleford to St Briavels road.</desc>
    <link href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/way/969564834"><text>OpenStreetMap way 969564834</text></link>
    <link href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Whitecliff_Ironworks"><text>Wikipedia: Whitecliff Ironworks</text></link>
    <sym>Ruins</sym>
    <type>Industrial</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="51.787020" lon="-2.633520">
    <ele>168</ele>
    <name>Dog Kennel Bridge</name>
    <desc>Stop 7. [Lost railway] Two generations of railway side by side. The 1883 Coleford Railway crosses the lane on Dog Kennel Bridge, with massive stone abutments and a brick arch, and about 100 m up the lane (SO 5632 1007) stand the smaller stone abutments of the 1812 Monmouth tramroad it replaced. The GWR branch closed in 1917 and most of its track was lifted for the war effort.</desc>
    <link href="https://forestofdeanhistory.org.uk/learn-about-the-forest/dog-kennel-bridge/"><text>FoD Local History Society: Dog Kennel Bridge</text></link>
    <link href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/way/553650248"><text>OpenStreetMap way 553650248</text></link>
    <sym>Bridge</sym>
    <type>Lost railway</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="51.782600" lon="-2.649770">
    <ele>122</ele>
    <name>Newland miner's brass</name>
    <desc>Stop 8. [Church curiosity] In All Saints', the 'Cathedral of the Forest', the 15th-century Greyndour brass carries a unique crest: a Forest free miner with a hod on his back, a pick in his hand and a candle held in his mouth. The church is usually open in daylight.</desc>
    <link href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/way/164444665"><text>OpenStreetMap way 164444665</text></link>
    <sym>Church</sym>
    <type>Church curiosity</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="51.789040" lon="-2.673020">
    <ele>51</ele>
    <name>Redbrook incline bridge</name>
    <desc>Stop 9. [Lost railway] Stone bridge on the self-acting, rope-worked incline of the Monmouth tramroad's Redbrook branch (opened 1812), which lowered coal to the Redbrook tinplate works and a wharf on the Wye. It crosses the Redbrook to Newland road, which the England-Wales border runs along.</desc>
    <link href="https://forestofdeanhistory.org.uk/learn-about-the-forest/redbrook-incline/"><text>FoD Local History Society: Redbrook Incline</text></link>
    <link href="https://historicengland.org.uk/listing/the-list/list-entry/1299263"><text>Historic England 1299263</text></link>
    <link href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/way/694516543"><text>OpenStreetMap way 694516543</text></link>
    <sym>Bridge</sym>
    <type>Lost railway</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="51.805430" lon="-2.640570">
    <ele>219</ele>
    <name>Staunton Longstone</name>
    <desc>Stop 10. [Folklore] An 8 ft pillar of conglomerate beside the A4136 east of Staunton, possibly a Bronze Age standing stone. A 19th-century tale says it bleeds if you prick it at midnight. Fast road: keep to the verge.</desc>
    <link href="https://www.megalithic.co.uk/article.php?sid=8071"><text>Megalithic Portal: Staunton Longstone</text></link>
    <sym>Scenic Area</sym>
    <type>Folklore</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="51.810180" lon="-2.659640">
    <ele>215</ele>
    <name>Toad's Mouth</name>
    <desc>Stop 11. [Folklore] Weathered rock outcrop at the west end of Staunton, opposite an old toll cottage, whose big hollow looks like a toad's open mouth when seen from the north. One of Staunton's 'notable stones'. Roadside on the A4136: mind the traffic.</desc>
    <link href="https://www.megalithic.co.uk/article.php?sid=31510"><text>Megalithic Portal: Toad's Mouth</text></link>
    <sym>Scenic Area</sym>
    <type>Folklore</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="51.807209" lon="-2.666233">
    <ele>267</ele>
    <name>Buck Stone</name>
    <desc>Stop 12. [Folklore] A logan (rocking) stone that could be rocked by hand until 1885, when a party of visitors, said to be travelling actors, pushed it off its perch. It was hauled back and cemented in place and has not rocked since. Folklore links it to Druid rites; big views over the Wye into Wales.</desc>
    <link href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/node/347430899"><text>OpenStreetMap node 347430899</text></link>
    <link href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Buck_Stone"><text>Wikipedia: Buck Stone</text></link>
    <sym>Scenic Area</sym>
    <type>Folklore</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="51.821664" lon="-2.664065">
    <ele>165</ele>
    <name>Near Hearkening Rock</name>
    <desc>Stop 13. [Folklore] Conglomerate crag said to take its name from gamekeepers who listened ('hearkened') here for poachers: the rock face throws sound up from the valley. Far Hearkening Rock is nearby. Unfenced edges.</desc>
    <link href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/way/166409053"><text>OpenStreetMap way 166409053</text></link>
    <sym>Scenic Area</sym>
    <type>Folklore</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="51.822988" lon="-2.665809">
    <ele>154</ele>
    <name>Suck Stone</name>
    <desc>Stop 14. [Folklore] Claimed to be the largest single boulder in Britain, with estimates from 4,000 to 14,000 tonnes. The block of quartz conglomerate broke away from the crag above and lies half-hidden in woods below Near Hearkening Rock. Boulderers climb it; the path down is steep and rough.</desc>
    <link href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/node/347430712"><text>OpenStreetMap node 347430712</text></link>
    <link href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Suck_Stone"><text>Wikipedia: Suck Stone</text></link>
    <sym>Scenic Area</sym>
    <type>Folklore</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="51.846770" lon="-2.610500">
    <ele>26</ele>
    <name>Halifax crash memorial</name>
    <desc>Stop 15. [WWII] On 7 June 1942 Halifax V9977, secretly testing the new H2S airborne radar, crashed here and all 11 aboard were killed, among them Alan Blumlein, inventor of stereo sound. A stone memorial unveiled in 2019 stands beside the Wye Valley Walk on the Courtfield estate, across the river from Lydbrook (Herefordshire bank).</desc>
    <link href="https://memorials.iwm.org.uk/memorial/84788"><text>IWM War Memorials Register 84788</text></link>
    <link href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1942_Herefordshire_TRE_Halifax_crash"><text>Wikipedia: 1942 TRE Halifax crash</text></link>
    <link href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/node/11135114575"><text>OpenStreetMap node 11135114575</text></link>
    <sym>Monument</sym>
    <type>WWII</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="51.856350" lon="-2.601070">
    <ele>40</ele>
    <name>Lydbrook pillbox</name>
    <desc>Stop 16. [WWII] WWII pillbox guarding the north end of the old Ross-Monmouth railway bridge over the Wye, now a footbridge on the Wye Valley Walk, and the south end of the bricked-up tunnel under Coppett Hill. On the Herefordshire bank, just across the river from Lydbrook. Pin accurate to about 100 m.</desc>
    <link href="https://www.geograph.org.uk/photo/144897"><text>Geograph 144897</text></link>
    <link href="https://www.geograph.org.uk/photo/724564"><text>Geograph 724564</text></link>
    <sym>Building</sym>
    <type>WWII</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="51.849749" lon="-2.572673">
    <ele>132</ele>
    <name>Lydbrook viaduct site</name>
    <desc>Stop 17. [Lost railway] George Keeling's Severn &amp; Wye viaduct carried the Lydbrook branch across Lower Lydbrook on masonry arches and two 90 ft piers with three iron girder spans (120, 150 and 120 ft), opened for goods in 1874. Passengers stopped in 1929, goods in 1956, and it was demolished in 1965. Only overgrown parts of the stone abutments remain; Viaduct Cottage and Viaduct House once stood almost beneath it. A row of four old limekilns stands about 250 m south-west, on the bank above Vention Road. Pin accurate to about 100 m.</desc>
    <link href="https://forestofdeanhistory.org.uk/learn-about-the-forest/lydbrook-railway-viaduct/"><text>FoD Local History Society: Lydbrook Railway Viaduct</text></link>
    <sym>Bridge</sym>
    <type>Lost railway</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="51.856370" lon="-2.551480">
    <ele>197</ele>
    <name>Ruardean fish &amp; castle</name>
    <desc>Stop 18. [Church curiosity] St John the Baptist's has a Norman tympanum of St George and the dragon carved about 1150 by the Herefordshire School, plus a stone of two carved fish by the same masons, rediscovered in a nearby cottage wall and now set inside. Beside the churchyard are the mounds and wall fragments of Ruardean Castle.</desc>
    <link href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/way/414714334"><text>OpenStreetMap way 414714334</text></link>
    <link href="https://historicengland.org.uk/listing/the-list/list-entry/1100629"><text>Historic England 1100629</text></link>
    <sym>Church</sym>
    <type>Church curiosity</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="51.870400" lon="-2.502250">
    <ele>284</ele>
    <name>Pingry's Tump gallows</name>
    <desc>Stop 19. [Folklore] A mound on Wigpool Common remembered as a gallows site: local history records that Eli Hatton was hanged here in 1723 for murdering a carpenter, Tom Twibervile. Around it lie the scowles and shafts of Wigpool iron mine, now an SSSI where horseshoe bats hibernate; Pit House Pond was dug in 1840 as the mine's reservoir. Keep out of any mine entrances.</desc>
    <link href="https://www.geograph.org.uk/photo/12732"><text>Geograph 12732</text></link>
    <link href="https://www.geograph.org.uk/photo/3876955"><text>Geograph 3876955</text></link>
    <sym>Flag, Red</sym>
    <type>Folklore</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="51.849193" lon="-2.497174">
    <ele>244</ele>
    <name>Fairplay engine house</name>
    <desc>Stop 20. [Mining] Best-preserved iron-mine engine house in the Forest: a Cornish underbeam engine house up to 15 ft high over a 350 ft masonry shaft, sunk from about 1856. About 100 yards east is a filled-in AIR SHAFT with the flue and base of its ventilation chimney, hearth and all, conserved in 1980. The little ponds south of the shafts held boiler water and are now nature reserves. The mine seems never to have produced much ore. By the road near Fairplay Cottages, Plump Hill. Pin accurate to about 100 m.</desc>
    <link href="https://forestofdeanhistory.org.uk/learn-about-the-forest/fairplay-iron-mine-engine-house/"><text>FoD Local History Society: Fairplay Iron Mine Engine House</text></link>
    <sym>Mine</sym>
    <type>Mining</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="51.845990" lon="-2.491230">
    <ele>259</ele>
    <name>Westbury Brook iron mine</name>
    <desc>Stop 21. [Mining] The Dowlais Company worked this mine from 1837 to 1893 and raised almost a million tons of iron ore through a 360 ft 'Old shaft' and a later, deeper shaft. Today a fenced entrance and a concrete-capped shaft sit by Edgehills Quarry, whose face shows 75 m of Drybrook Sandstone. The workings are an SSSI where lesser and greater horseshoe bats hibernate. Two ruined limekilns stand about 550 m north. View from outside the fence only.</desc>
    <link href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/node/857172926"><text>OpenStreetMap node 857172926</text></link>
    <sym>Mine</sym>
    <type>Mining</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="51.839407" lon="-2.480186">
    <ele>136</ele>
    <name>St Anthony's Well</name>
    <desc>Stop 22. [Folklore] A cold spring in the woods above Flaxley, traditionally linked with the Cistercian monks of Flaxley Abbey. Folklore says bathing in it on nine May mornings in a row cured skin diseases, and that it cured mange in dogs. The water stays icy even in summer.</desc>
    <link href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/node/885547118"><text>OpenStreetMap node 885547118</text></link>
    <sym>Water Source</sym>
    <type>Folklore</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="51.838853" lon="-2.471189">
    <ele>128</ele>
    <name>Welshbury hillfort</name>
    <desc>Stop 23. [Ancient site] Iron Age hillfort with up to three lines of ramparts, hidden under trees on Welshbury Hill. Ancient coppiced small-leaved limes grow along the banks. Scheduled monument: no digging or metal detecting.</desc>
    <link href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/way/29958958"><text>OpenStreetMap way 29958958</text></link>
    <link href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Welshbury_Hill"><text>Wikipedia: Welshbury Hill</text></link>
    <sym>Ruins</sym>
    <type>Ancient site</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="51.819160" lon="-2.470630">
    <ele>171</ele>
    <name>Littledean Camp</name>
    <desc>Stop 24. [Ancient site] Circular earthwork castle of the late 11th or early 12th century, called 'the old castle of Dene' in the 12th century and said to have given the Forest of Dean its name. It stands about 600 ft up with views over the Severn. Scheduled monument, given to the Dean Heritage Museum Trust by the forest historian Dr Cyril Hart in 1987. No public path onto it: it sits among private fields west of Littledean, so view it from the lane.</desc>
    <link href="https://forestofdeanhistory.org.uk/learn-about-the-forest/littledean-camp/"><text>FoD Local History Society: Littledean Camp</text></link>
    <sym>Ruins</sym>
    <type>Ancient site</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="51.833730" lon="-2.518050">
    <ele>168</ele>
    <name>New Bowson shaft collar</name>
    <desc>Stop 25. [Mining] The Forest's first attempt at deep mining. From 1864 the Great Western Deep Coal Co. sank two shafts here, one to 865 ft, but a second-hand 85-inch Cornish pumping engine failed in 1869, little coal was won and the site was abandoned in 1925. A restored circular shaft collar, engine-house footings and an information board survive in Cinderford Linear Park.</desc>
    <link href="https://www.geograph.org.uk/photo/6492129"><text>Geograph 6492129</text></link>
    <sym>Mine</sym>
    <type>Mining</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="51.837550" lon="-2.528110">
    <ele>191</ele>
    <name>Last deep mine of Dean</name>
    <desc>Stop 26. [Mining] Northern United, sunk from May 1933, was the Forest's last deep colliery; it closed on Christmas Day 1965, ending deep mining in the Dean. A sculpture of a stylised shaft with carved sandstone miners stands on a base lettered 'NORTHERN UNITED LAST DEEP MINE OF DEAN', with tram rails, a weighbridge and derelict pithead baths nearby. It is in a gated recycling yard: view from outside.</desc>
    <link href="https://www.geograph.org.uk/photo/6492048"><text>Geograph 6492048</text></link>
    <sym>Monument</sym>
    <type>Mining</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="51.825550" lon="-2.546430">
    <ele>175</ele>
    <name>Trafalgar Arch</name>
    <desc>Stop 27. [Lost railway] Brick-lined stone arch the Severn &amp; Wye built about 1878, for about GBP 200, to brace its retaining wall against the spoil heap of Trafalgar Colliery; it was restored when the trackbed became a cycleway. Trafalgar was lit by gas, and in 1882 it put electric pumps underground, the first recorded use of electric power in a mine. A green-plaque stone 130 m north honours Sir Francis Brain (1855-1921), whose family owned the pit and pioneered electric detonators. Two big boulders about 70 m north-west mark the filled twin shafts.</desc>
    <link href="https://forestofdeanhistory.org.uk/learn-about-the-forest/trafalgar-arch/"><text>FoD Local History Society: Trafalgar Arch</text></link>
    <link href="https://forestofdeanhistory.org.uk/learn-about-the-forest/trafalgar-colliery/"><text>FoD Local History Society: Trafalgar Colliery</text></link>
    <link href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/way/1271928302"><text>OpenStreetMap way 1271928302</text></link>
    <sym>Bridge</sym>
    <type>Lost railway</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="51.828970" lon="-2.556010">
    <ele>157</ele>
    <name>Waterloo pit memorial</name>
    <desc>Stop 28. [Mining] On 30 June 1949 miners at Waterloo (Arthur &amp; Edward) Colliery broke into the flooded workings of East Slad Colliery. 177 men escaped up the shaft in the cage and five more were rescued through the old Pluds' shaft. An inscribed memorial and a pool mark the site; the pit closed in December 1959. Deep water: keep back from the pool.</desc>
    <link href="https://www.geograph.org.uk/photo/7339404"><text>Geograph 7339404</text></link>
    <sym>Monument</sym>
    <type>Mining</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="51.828210" lon="-2.560610">
    <ele>143</ele>
    <name>Mierystock tunnel</name>
    <desc>Stop 29. [Lost railway] From the stone Mirystock bridge on the Family Cycle Trail, look NNE along the wet trackbed: the dressed-stone south portal is about 80 m away. The 242-yard tunnel saw a ganger killed in 1893 and a rockfall derailment in 1898; the last train, a demolition working, ran on 11 July 1960. In the 1970s the north cutting was filled with 30,000 tonnes of colliery waste; volunteers dug down to the keystone in 2005 and re-dug the cutting in 2007 with People's Millions money. Don't enter.</desc>
    <link href="http://www.forgottenrelics.org/tunnels/mierystock-tunnel/"><text>Forgotten Relics: Mierystock Tunnel</text></link>
    <link href="https://www.geograph.org.uk/photo/6370739"><text>Geograph 6370739</text></link>
    <sym>Tunnel</sym>
    <type>Lost railway</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="51.802000" lon="-2.595500">
    <ele>222</ele>
    <name>Ammo depot camp (WWII)</name>
    <desc>Stop 30. [WWII] Concrete hut bases and paths in the woods mark the camp built from 1940 for No. 28 Ammunition Sub-Depot, which stacked munitions along the Forest roads around Speech House until the store covered some 36 square miles. About 900 US soldiers worked here from 1943, and German POWs were held at the end of the war; volunteers recorded 30 structures in 2021. The pin is an estimate: search the woodland edge where Woodville Avenue meets Worcester Walk.</desc>
    <link href="https://www.forestersforest.uk/news/article/74/unearthing-ww2-camp-remains"><text>Foresters' Forest: WW2 camp remains</text></link>
    <sym>Building</sym>
    <type>WWII</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="51.796149" lon="-2.587656">
    <ele>213</ele>
    <name>Bixhead stone mine</name>
    <desc>Stop 31. [Mining] Quarries and a stone mine at the head of Bixslade, worked for centuries for grey-blue Pennant 'Forest stone'. The stone-mine entrance is barred: do not enter. Quarry faces are unstable and some quarries are still worked, so keep to the tracks.</desc>
    <link href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/node/2246199865"><text>OpenStreetMap node 2246199865</text></link>
    <sym>Mine</sym>
    <type>Mining</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="51.787393" lon="-2.577250">
    <ele>108</ele>
    <name>Union Pit memorial</name>
    <desc>Stop 32. [Mining] On 4 September 1902 water from old workings flooded Union Pit, trapping seven men. Three came out alive after five days; Herbert Gwatkin, Thomas James, Amos James and William Martin drowned. Matt Baker's sculpture 'Still There Were Brothers', carved from 10 tonnes of local Pennant sandstone for the Royal Forest of Dean Freeminers Association, marks the spot. Monument Mine, a working free mine named after the memorial, is close by: private, keep out.</desc>
    <link href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/node/3700967265"><text>OpenStreetMap node 3700967265</text></link>
    <link href="https://forestofdeanhistory.org.uk/learn-about-the-forest/union-colliery-disaster-memorial/"><text>Forest of Dean Local History Society</text></link>
    <link href="https://www.geograph.org.uk/photo/4268705"><text>Geograph 4268705</text></link>
    <sym>Monument</sym>
    <type>Mining</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="51.786815" lon="-2.571875">
    <ele>94</ele>
    <name>Bixslade tramroad rails</name>
    <desc>Stop 33. [Lost railway] The Bixslade branch of the Severn &amp; Wye tramroad opened by 1812 and carried stone and coal until 1946. Its stone sleeper blocks, 14 in square and at least 160 lb, still line the lower valley, some with the spike holes of the original L-shaped iron plates. Near the 1901 Bixslade stone works lies the only stretch of tramroad track known to survive here. Pin accurate to about 100 m.</desc>
    <link href="https://forestofdeanhistory.org.uk/learn-about-the-forest/bixlade-tramway-rails/"><text>FoD Local History Society: Bixslade tramway rails</text></link>
    <link href="https://forestofdeanhistory.org.uk/learn-about-the-forest/bixlade-tramway-blocks/"><text>FoD Local History Society: Bixslade tramway blocks</text></link>
    <sym>Crossing</sym>
    <type>Lost railway</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="51.795051" lon="-2.541529">
    <ele>155</ele>
    <name>Reform Bridge (1833)</name>
    <desc>Stop 34. [Curiosity] An old forest bridge south of Speech House Lake whose northern arch carries a stone carved 'Reform 1833', thought to celebrate the Great Reform Act of 1832, which created the West Gloucestershire constituency. Look for the carved stone on the north side. About 200 m east, the Mineral Loop crosses a ride on 'Central Bridge', named after the rival Forest of Dean Central Railway, which never got this far. Pin accurate to about 100 m.</desc>
    <link href="https://forestofdeanhistory.org.uk/learn-about-the-forest/reform-bridge/"><text>FoD Local History Society: Reform Bridge</text></link>
    <sym>Bridge</sym>
    <type>Curiosity</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="51.806860" lon="-2.552496">
    <ele>176</ele>
    <name>Centre of the Forest</name>
    <desc>Stop 35. [Curiosity] A stone near the Speech House marks the traditional centre of the Forest of Dean. The Speech House, built in 1676 as a hunting lodge and forest court, still hosts the Verderers' Court, one of England's oldest surviving courts.</desc>
    <link href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/node/9118443341"><text>OpenStreetMap node 9118443341</text></link>
    <link href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Speech_House"><text>Wikipedia: Speech House</text></link>
    <sym>Flag, Red</sym>
    <type>Curiosity</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="51.818400" lon="-2.527300">
    <ele>200</ele>
    <name>Foxes Bridge pump bob</name>
    <desc>Stop 36. [Mining] The inverted-T 'bob' of a steam pumping engine lies on its concrete pad by the cycle trail, with winding-engine footings and masonry close by. Foxes Bridge, sunk from 1855 to 851 ft, was one of the Dean's biggest pits until water from the abandoned Crump Meadow workings forced it to close in 1930. Watch for cyclists.</desc>
    <link href="https://www.geograph.org.uk/photo/6946071"><text>Geograph 6946071</text></link>
    <sym>Mine</sym>
    <type>Mining</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="51.806050" lon="-2.527492">
    <ele>187</ele>
    <name>Lightmoor engine house</name>
    <desc>Stop 37. [Mining] Stone engine house and buildings of Lightmoor Colliery, one of the Forest's big deep pits, which worked until 1940. They are among the best-surviving colliery buildings in the Dean. Private yard: view from the road or track.</desc>
    <link href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/node/347203479"><text>OpenStreetMap node 347203479</text></link>
    <sym>Mine</sym>
    <type>Mining</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="51.792950" lon="-2.507430">
    <ele>193</ele>
    <name>Findall's air chimney</name>
    <desc>Stop 38. [Mining] A complete 45 ft stone ventilation chimney halfway down a scowle in beech woods at Staple Edge. A fire in the firebox at its base drew foul air up out of Findall iron mine through stone flues. The mine, worked from 1841 by Edward Protheroe and William Crawshay, fed Soudley furnace and joined Perseverance mine underground; its machinery was sold in 1907. Conserved in 1976-77, with three small boiler ponds nearby. Steep, slippery scowle sides: don't climb it or go into the flues.</desc>
    <link href="https://www.geograph.org.uk/photo/1024177"><text>Geograph 1024177</text></link>
    <link href="https://www.geograph.org.uk/photo/1019376"><text>Geograph 1019376</text></link>
    <sym>Mine</sym>
    <type>Mining</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="51.792870" lon="-2.491980">
    <ele>102</ele>
    <name>Soudley Camp</name>
    <desc>Stop 39. [Ancient site] Small Iron Age enclosure on a steep spur above a bend in Soudley Brook: one rampart up to 2 m high with a 9 m wide outer ditch on the west, steep natural slopes elsewhere and a single central entrance. Scheduled monument. Bracken, ticks and steep ground.</desc>
    <link href="https://historicengland.org.uk/listing/the-list/list-entry/1005564"><text>Historic England 1005564</text></link>
    <sym>Ruins</sym>
    <type>Ancient site</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="51.789270" lon="-2.486800">
    <ele>75</ele>
    <name>Haie Hill tunnel (1809)</name>
    <desc>Stop 40. [Lost railway] Stand by the surviving gatepost of the old Soudley No. 1 level crossing on public bridleway ENE 32: the bricked-up west portal, with a stream running out beneath it, is about 55 m east. Built for the horse-drawn Bullo Pill tramroad authorised in 1809, the 1,083-yard tunnel is said to have been the world's longest when it opened. Brunel enlarged it for broad gauge in 1854 while trains kept running, and it closed on 1 August 1967. The east end is on private land.</desc>
    <link href="http://www.forgottenrelics.org/tunnels/haie-hill-tunnel/"><text>Forgotten Relics: Haie Hill Tunnel</text></link>
    <link href="https://www.geograph.org.uk/photo/6507066"><text>Geograph 6507066</text></link>
    <link href="https://www.geograph.org.uk/photo/6502512"><text>Geograph 6502512</text></link>
    <sym>Tunnel</sym>
    <type>Lost railway</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="51.786810" lon="-2.450240">
    <ele>13</ele>
    <name>Bullo Pill dock</name>
    <desc>Stop 41. [Industrial] Tidal dock at the Severn end of the 1809 Bullo Pill tramroad, the line that ran through Haie Hill tunnel. At its peak more than 1,000 tons of Forest coal and stone a day left from here; the railway took its trade by 1900 and it is now silted. See it from the riverside footpath.</desc>
    <link href="https://www.geograph.org.uk/photo/2488355"><text>Geograph 2488355</text></link>
    <sym>Anchor</sym>
    <type>Industrial</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="51.770310" lon="-2.423420">
    <ele>20</ele>
    <name>Awre mortuary chest</name>
    <desc>Stop 42. [Church curiosity] Under the tower of Grade I St Andrew's church stands a huge chest hollowed from a single tree trunk, probably yew. By tradition it was used to lay out the bodies of people drowned in the Severn, which curls round the parish; drowned fishermen are said to lie beneath the ancient churchyard yew. The church is usually open in daylight.</desc>
    <link href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/way/237743278"><text>OpenStreetMap way 237743278</text></link>
    <link href="https://forestofdeanhistory.org.uk/learn-about-the-forest/st-andrews-church-awre/"><text>FoD Local History Society: St Andrew's Church Awre</text></link>
    <sym>Church</sym>
    <type>Church curiosity</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="51.778410" lon="-2.501900">
    <ele>117</ele>
    <name>Drummer Boy Stone</name>
    <desc>Stop 43. [Folklore] A big block of conglomerate beside the brook about 350 yards north of Blackpool Bridge, with two man-made hollows, one still holding iron fused to the stone; bits of slag lie in the stream. Archaeologists read it as a mortar stone for crushing iron ore. The name hints at a drummer boy's grave, but no burial has ever been found.</desc>
    <link href="https://www.megalithic.co.uk/article.php?sid=24510"><text>Megalithic Portal: Drummer Boy Stone</text></link>
    <sym>Scenic Area</sym>
    <type>Folklore</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="51.775659" lon="-2.505659">
    <ele>90</ele>
    <name>The Dean Road</name>
    <desc>Stop 44. [Ancient site] A stretch of kerbed, stone-paved road beside Blackpool Brook, long called a Roman road. Its date is debated: it may follow a Roman route, but many now think the surface is medieval or even 18th century. Scheduled monument; please don't lift the stones.</desc>
    <link href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/node/415999285"><text>OpenStreetMap node 415999285</text></link>
    <sym>Scenic Area</sym>
    <type>Ancient site</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="51.776614" lon="-2.523261">
    <ele>111</ele>
    <name>Morse's Level</name>
    <desc>Stop 45. [Mining] Coal level driven about 1832 by George Morse, a Yorkley free miner, running 900 yards south into the Yorkley seam; it was worked again as a free mine in the 1990s. The stone drift entrance is gated, with tramway rails running into the dark. The concrete footings and capped shaft of Howbeach Colliery (closed in the 1920s) are about 100 m away. Do not enter.</desc>
    <link href="https://forestofdeanhistory.org.uk/learn-about-the-forest/morses-level/"><text>FoD Local History Society: Morse's Level</text></link>
    <link href="https://forestofdeanhistory.org.uk/learn-about-the-forest/howbeach-colliery/"><text>FoD Local History Society: Howbeach Colliery</text></link>
    <link href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/node/13953782501"><text>OpenStreetMap node 13953782501</text></link>
    <sym>Mine</sym>
    <type>Mining</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="51.773330" lon="-2.534460">
    <ele>123</ele>
    <name>Moseley Green tunnel</name>
    <desc>Stop 46. [Lost railway] The Severn &amp; Wye's Mineral Loop tunnel: 503 yards, masonry-lined, with three ventilation shafts (one capped), opened in the early 1870s. In WWII it was an ammunition store from April 1942 to December 1943, and it closed to all traffic on 13 March 1951. The north portal hides in the vegetation at the end of the old trackbed, a few metres from the lane. The far seal is broken and the bracing inside has been burnt: view only.</desc>
    <link href="http://www.forgottenrelics.org/tunnels/moseley-green-tunnel/"><text>Forgotten Relics: Moseley Green Tunnel</text></link>
    <link href="https://www.geograph.org.uk/photo/5997035"><text>Geograph 5997035</text></link>
    <sym>Tunnel</sym>
    <type>Lost railway</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="51.770440" lon="-2.536310">
    <ele>133</ele>
    <name>Tunnel air shaft</name>
    <desc>Stop 47. [Lost railway] The southernmost of the ventilation shafts of Moseley Green railway tunnel (1870s), rising from the tunnel about 350 m south of its north portal. The tunnel below was an ammunition store in WWII. Don't climb on the shaft head or reach through any grille.</desc>
    <link href="https://www.geograph.org.uk/photo/2804044"><text>Geograph 2804044</text></link>
    <sym>Tunnel</sym>
    <type>Lost railway</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="51.741830" lon="-2.478620">
    <ele>32</ele>
    <name>Railway that never ran</name>
    <desc>Stop 48. [Lost railway] An arch of a three-arch viaduct for the Purton Steam Carriage Road, an early-1830s scheme for a line from Purton Pill to Foxes Bridge colliery. Promoter Charles Mathias bought land and started building before the 1832 Bill had passed; the Commissioners of Woods opposed it and it was dropped. Part or all of Nibley Hill tunnel near Blakeney belongs to the same lost line.</desc>
    <link href="https://www.geograph.org.uk/photo/6503290"><text>Geograph 6503290</text></link>
    <sym>Bridge</sym>
    <type>Lost railway</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="51.732970" lon="-2.493770">
    <ele>51</ele>
    <name>Severn Bridge tunnel</name>
    <desc>Stop 49. [Lost railway] The 506-yard tunnel (1875-78) on the west-bank approach to the Severn Railway Bridge. The line was cut on 25 October 1960 when two fuel barges hit the bridge in fog; two rail tours ran through in 1964 and the track was lifted in 1968-69. This moss-covered south-west portal has its own little stream. The platform edges of Severn Bridge station survive near the north-east end, on private land. View only.</desc>
    <link href="http://www.forgottenrelics.org/tunnels/severn-bridge-tunnel/"><text>Forgotten Relics: Severn Bridge Tunnel</text></link>
    <link href="https://www.geograph.org.uk/photo/3861607"><text>Geograph 3861607</text></link>
    <sym>Tunnel</sym>
    <type>Lost railway</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="51.716080" lon="-2.508450">
    <ele>23</ele>
    <name>Naas Lane POW camp</name>
    <desc>Stop 50. [WWII] WWII camp. In 1939 the Ministry of Supply took land beside Lydney canal; 35 concrete and Nissen huts housed 400 troops and the German and Italian POWs who worked at the depot and on farms. The US Army's 3188 Quartermaster Service Company left in 1946, then families lived here as 'The Avenue' until 1957. Four overgrown huts and the stripped water tower survive by Naas Court. View from the public footpath; the landowner's permission is needed to go closer.</desc>
    <link href="https://www.sungreen.co.uk/Lydney/Naas_Camp.html"><text>SunGreen: Naas Lane POW camp</text></link>
    <link href="https://www.geograph.org.uk/photo/2795994"><text>Geograph 2795994</text></link>
    <link href="https://www.derelictplaces.co.uk/threads/small-army-camp-near-lydney-march-09.9892/"><text>Derelict Places forum thread</text></link>
    <sym>Building</sym>
    <type>WWII</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="51.710420" lon="-2.506070">
    <ele>11</ele>
    <name>1960 disaster plaque</name>
    <desc>Stop 51. [Memorial] At the mouth of Lydney harbour a plaque remembers the five men who died on 25 October 1960, when the tanker barges Arkendale H and Wastdale H hit the Severn Railway Bridge in fog and brought two spans down. The harbour is a scheduled monument; its lock was restored in 2003-05. A former limekiln stands beside the lock.</desc>
    <link href="https://www.geograph.org.uk/photo/3683187"><text>Geograph 3683187</text></link>
    <link href="https://www.geograph.org.uk/photo/5333594"><text>Geograph 5333594</text></link>
    <sym>Monument</sym>
    <type>Memorial</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="51.709670" lon="-2.514020">
    <ele>13</ele>
    <name>Pine End Works (WWII)</name>
    <desc>Stop 52. [WWII] Government shadow factory built in 1940 to make plywood for de Havilland Mosquito bombers and Horsa gliders; logs arrived by barge and a gantry crane swung them across Harbour Road. It worked for over 60 years and is now a cleared site with one brick building. Next door was a big US Army Quartermaster depot whose camouflaged sheds and huts still stood in 2007. Private: view from Harbour Road.</desc>
    <link href="https://gsia.org.uk/sites/files/nq/gsianq_lydney_pine_end_works_20200722.pdf"><text>GSIA: Pine End Works</text></link>
    <link href="https://www.geograph.org.uk/photo/3682891"><text>Geograph 3682891</text></link>
    <sym>Building</sym>
    <type>WWII</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="51.708930" lon="-2.517780">
    <ele>8</ele>
    <name>Octagonal pillbox</name>
    <desc>Stop 53. [WWII] A rare design: an octagonal WWII pillbox of reinforced concrete with eight gun loops, no central anti-aircraft well and a blast wall shielding its low west entrance. It stands beside the footpath from Lydney harbour to the railway station. Pin accurate to about 100 m.</desc>
    <link href="https://www.geograph.org.uk/photo/1894810"><text>Geograph 1894810</text></link>
    <link href="https://www.geograph.org.uk/photo/1327445"><text>Geograph 1327445</text></link>
    <sym>Building</sym>
    <type>WWII</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="51.711330" lon="-2.525040">
    <ele>10</ele>
    <name>Type 24 pillbox, Lydney</name>
    <desc>Stop 54. [WWII] Hexagonal WWII Type 24 pillbox, one of at least two concrete strongpoints guarding the approaches to Lydney's docks. Seen from the canal-side path between the harbour and the station. Don't go inside.</desc>
    <link href="https://www.geograph.org.uk/photo/5169313"><text>Geograph 5169313</text></link>
    <sym>Building</sym>
    <type>WWII</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="51.689690" lon="-2.576150">
    <ele>14</ele>
    <name>Railway pillbox line</name>
    <desc>Stop 55. [WWII] Three prefabricated Type 26 pillboxes from 1940 stand in a line along the Gloucester-Chepstow railway near the Plusterwine level crossing; a fourth sits above a small quarry near Woolaston. View from the public footpath crossing only: this is a live main line. Pin accurate to about 100 m.</desc>
    <link href="https://www.geograph.org.uk/photo/740018"><text>Geograph 740018</text></link>
    <link href="https://www.geograph.org.uk/photo/740044"><text>Geograph 740044</text></link>
    <link href="https://www.geograph.org.uk/photo/740077"><text>Geograph 740077</text></link>
    <sym>Building</sym>
    <type>WWII</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="51.641700" lon="-2.656610">
    <ele>38</ele>
    <name>WWI POW camp huts</name>
    <desc>Stop 56. [WWI] Huts said to survive from the WWI prisoner-of-war camp linked to the failed National Shipyard at Beachley; the Army boys' technical school later opened in the old camp. Last photographed in 2010, so they may have gone. View from Grahamstown Road only; Beachley barracks is an active MOD site. Pin accurate to about 100 m.</desc>
    <link href="https://tidenhamhistory.co.uk/sedbury/"><text>Tidenham History Group: Sedbury</text></link>
    <link href="https://www.geograph.org.uk/photo/1940922"><text>Geograph 1940922</text></link>
    <sym>Building</sym>
    <type>WWI</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="51.663090" lon="-2.663660">
    <ele>75</ele>
    <name>Wintour's Leap</name>
    <desc>Stop 57. [Folklore] Sheer limestone cliffs above a loop of the Wye. Legend says the Royalist Sir John Wintour of Lydney escaped Parliamentarian troops during the Civil War by riding his horse over the edge and swimming the river. Unfenced drop: keep well back.</desc>
    <link href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/node/6612200282"><text>OpenStreetMap node 6612200282</text></link>
    <link href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wintour%27s_Leap"><text>Wikipedia: Wintour's Leap</text></link>
    <sym>Scenic Area</sym>
    <type>Folklore</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="51.665180" lon="-2.670761">
    <ele>49</ele>
    <name>Lancaut ruined church</name>
    <desc>Stop 58. [Ancient site] The roofless 12th-century church of St James, all that is left of the lost village of Lancaut on a bend of the Wye. Its 12th-century lead font was moved to Gloucester Cathedral. Reached on foot down a steep path from Lancaut Lane, where there is a small parking area. The ruin of an old limekiln lies about 190 m north-north-east.</desc>
    <link href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/way/132153949"><text>OpenStreetMap way 132153949</text></link>
    <link href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lancaut"><text>Wikipedia: Lancaut</text></link>
    <sym>Ruins</sym>
    <type>Ancient site</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="51.669330" lon="-2.653570">
    <ele>115</ele>
    <name>Tidenham air shaft</name>
    <desc>Stop 59. [Lost railway] Air shaft for the Wye Valley Railway's Tidenham tunnel (opened 1876, about 1,100 m long), sunk during construction about 490 yards from the north portal and mostly unlined; you can look down on it from Tidenham Chase. The tunnel below is now part of the Wye Valley Greenway, open to walkers and cyclists from April to September and closed October to March for hibernating horseshoe bats. Look, don't climb.</desc>
    <link href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/node/860709875"><text>OpenStreetMap node 860709875</text></link>
    <link href="http://www.forgottenrelics.org/tunnels/tidenham-tunnel/"><text>Forgotten Relics: Tidenham Tunnel</text></link>
    <link href="https://forestofdeanhistory.org.uk/learn-about-the-forest/tidenham-tunnel/"><text>FoD Local History Society: Tidenham Tunnel</text></link>
    <sym>Tunnel</sym>
    <type>Lost railway</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="51.680330" lon="-2.639040">
    <ele>185</ele>
    <name>Hidden Jubilee stone</name>
    <desc>Stop 60. [Curiosity] A stone dated 1897 for Queen Victoria's Diamond Jubilee, hidden among the trees of Parson's Allotment on Tidenham Chase, land given to the vicar in lieu of tithes in 1815. It toppled and was set up again by locals; an ancient mound lies about 25 m away.</desc>
    <link href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/node/11295852236"><text>OpenStreetMap node 11295852236</text></link>
    <link href="https://deangreenteam.co.uk/Diary-311017.html"><text>Dean Green Team diary</text></link>
    <sym>Monument</sym>
    <type>Curiosity</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="51.692550" lon="-2.662790">
    <ele>164</ele>
    <name>Devil's Pulpit</name>
    <desc>Stop 61. [Folklore] A limestone pillar on Offa's Dyke looking down on Tintern Abbey. Legend says the Devil preached from it to tempt the monks below; in some versions they drenched him with holy water. On the Offa's Dyke Path National Trail.</desc>
    <link href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/node/349159181"><text>OpenStreetMap node 349159181</text></link>
    <sym>Scenic Area</sym>
    <type>Folklore</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="51.738266" lon="-2.571262">
    <ele>171</ele>
    <name>Devil's Chapel scowles</name>
    <desc>Stop 62. [Mining] Scowles: deep, twisting, moss-lined chasms left by opencast iron-ore digging from Roman times or earlier, protected as a Site of Special Scientific Interest since 1998. The pin is the SSSI grid reference, good to about 100 m. Sheer, slippery drops: stay on paths and keep dogs and children close. Pin accurate to about 100 m.</desc>
    <link href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Devil%27s_Chapel_Scowles"><text>Wikipedia: Devil's Chapel Scowles</text></link>
    <sym>Mine</sym>
    <type>Mining</type>
  </wpt>
  <wpt lat="51.720913" lon="-2.557712">
    <ele>52</ele>
    <name>Dwarf's Hill temple</name>
    <desc>Stop 63. [Ancient site] Late-Roman temple to the healing god Nodens inside an Iron Age hillfort nicknamed Dwarf's Hill, riddled with Roman iron-mine workings. Mortimer Wheeler dug it in 1928-29 and J.R.R. Tolkien wrote the note on the name 'Nodens' for the 1932 report. A curse tablet found here asks Nodens to punish 'Senicianus' for stealing a ring; a gold ring bearing that name turned up at Silchester, a tale often linked to the One Ring. PRIVATE: Lydney Park estate, normally only open when the gardens open in spring.</desc>
    <link href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/way/236028496"><text>OpenStreetMap way 236028496</text></link>
    <link href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lydney_Park"><text>Wikipedia: Lydney Park</text></link>
    <sym>Ruins</sym>
    <type>Ancient site</type>
  </wpt>
  <trk>
    <name>Forest of Dean hidden places tour (from Lydney)</name>
    <desc>Tour through all 63 stops in order, starting and finishing in Lydney. Straight-line tour length 121 km.</desc>
    <type>hiking</type>
    <trkseg>
